# os/opensuse.leap.capabilities — the openSUSE LEAP capability declaration
# (Core v5, #663/#667).
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read (never sourced) by Core's zsh/02-capabilities.zsh into $_CORE_CAP, and by
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh, which is bash. bootstrap.sh links it to
# $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer.
#
# THE SOURCE of the "Package-manager commands" table in dotfiles-core's
# PORTING-MATRIX.md, openSUSE column
# (its Leap half; opensuse.capabilities is the Tumbleweed half) —
# the table is rendered from this file since dotfiles-core#686 (this checkout's vendored
# core/ copy may predate that and still say the reverse): edit here, then run
# `make gen-porting-matrix` in dotfiles-core.
# Validate with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/opensuse.leap.capabilities
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THIS FILE IS LEAP'S. TUMBLEWEED'S — THE DEFAULT — IS os/opensuse.capabilities.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ONE REPO SERVES TWO DISTRIBUTIONS THAT UPGRADE WITH DIFFERENT VERBS, and getting it
# wrong half-updates the box — it is the reason Core carried a
# `grep -qi tumbleweed /etc/os-release` probe in a portable module at all.
#
# A declaration is DATA: it cannot probe, so the choice has to be made somewhere. It
# is made in bootstrap.sh, which already reads /etc/os-release to decide which upgrade
# alias to advertise, and now relinks $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities to the Leap file on a
# non-Tumbleweed box. Tumbleweed is the default because it is what this repo primarily
# targets; a Leap box gets the other file, and a Leap→Tumbleweed migration is a
# re-bootstrap, which it already is for every other reason.
#
# THE FILES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. Three keys differ from the Tumbleweed file, and
# every difference is load-bearing — see PKG_UPGRADE, the scheduled-runner section and
# TOOLS_OPTIN below. Keep the rest in step by hand; there is deliberately no generator.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT — the reader keeps it. Notes go above.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=sudo zypper refresh
# `up`, NOT `dup` — THE FIRST OF THE THREE KEYS THAT DIFFER FROM THE TUMBLEWEED FILE.
# Leap is a versioned, stable release: `up` applies the maintenance updates it is
# meant to receive. `zypper dup` on Leap re-resolves the whole distribution against
# whatever repositories are enabled and is how you accidentally migrate a box.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo zypper up
PKG_INSTALL=sudo zypper in
PKG_REMOVE=sudo zypper rm
PKG_SEARCH=zypper se
PKG_OWNS=zypper se --provides
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=zypper -q list-updates

# ── what --packages must not nag about (#1087) ────────────────────────────────
# Same as Tumbleweed's: `zypper` is base-system on Leap too, so packages.txt does not
# name it and the cross-check would warn on every verb.
PKG_UNLISTED_TOOLS=zypper

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PKG_ASSUME_YES=-y
# Same verb as the full upgrade, narrowed to the named packages — which on a versioned
# release is an ordinary, supported thing to do.
PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=sudo zypper up
# NO PKG_UPGRADE_PRE: `up` refreshes as part of the transaction.
# NO PKG_CLEANUP: zypper does not accumulate the way apt and brew do.

# ── how `up` counts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `zypper list-updates` prints a PIPE-DELIMITED table: the `v` status column, then
# repository, then the package name in field 3. This is the only archive in the fleet
# whose output is not whitespace-separated.
PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^v[[:space:]]
PKG_PENDING_FS=|
PKG_PENDING_FIELD=3

# ── the scheduled runner ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DECLARED HERE AND DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE TUMBLEWEED FILE — THE SECOND KEY THAT
# DIFFERS, and the reason the tier split is not only about spelling the verb.
#
# The flag drives PKG_UPGRADE. On Leap that is `up`: maintenance updates to a versioned
# release, which is exactly what an unattended nightly is for, and exactly what this box
# does today (Core's untethered maint arm ran `zypper --non-interactive up` on any
# zypper box). On Tumbleweed the same flag would drive `dup` — an unattended
# DISTRIBUTION upgrade on a rolling distro — so that file omits it.
#
# The operator's MAINT_SYSTEM_UPGRADE=1 is still the FIRST of the two gates.
MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE=1

# ── optional tools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TOOLS_OPTIN. Same as the Tumbleweed file: this repo installs Core's opt-in set, so the
# list keeps only `ouch` (packaged, kept so the list is not empty) and then appends the
# names Leap cannot install. See that file for the full rationale.
#
# jj and ast-grep are packaged and so are expected here. jj's Leap build is old (0.22.0
# from Backports:SLE-16.x, checked 2026-10-11, against Tumbleweed's 0.46.0), but it
# installs, so it is expected rather than opt-in.
#
# `jc` IS APPENDED HERE AND NOT IN THE TUMBLEWEED FILE — THE THIRD KEY THAT DIFFERS
# (dotgibson/dotfiles-core#1208). Tumbleweed packages it; Leap 16.0 and 16.1 ship it in
# neither repo-oss nor Backports, zypper skips the name, and there is no Python tool
# installer on the box for a bootstrap fallback to use. So jc is optional HERE, not
# missing — without this word core-doctor would render a ✗ on every Leap box for a tool
# nothing will install. The `ouch` before it must match the Tumbleweed list exactly;
# test/check-flavors.sh enforces that. Drop the word if Leap ever packages jc.
#
# `difft` IS APPENDED FOR THE SAME REASON. Tumbleweed packages `difftastic`; Leap 16.0 and
# 16.1 ship it in neither repo-oss nor Backports:SLE-16.x (checked 2026-10-11). zypper skips
# the name, and a cargo build is not worth it for a tool Core wires only as the on-demand
# `git dft`. Drop the word if Leap ever packages difftastic.
#
# `lnav` IS APPENDED FOR THE SAME REASON. Tumbleweed packages it; Leap 16.0 and 16.1 ship it
# in neither repo-oss nor Backports:SLE-16.x (checked 2026-10-11). The rest of Core's opt-in
# set installs on Leap: gping, ShellCheck, shfmt, hyperfine, watchexec and git-absorb are
# packaged, python3-uv is provided by python313-uv, and bootstrap.sh cargo-builds jnv.
TOOLS_OPTIN=ouch jc difft lnav
